Ultimate Master Roadmap (2026+)
Software Engineering → AI/LLM Engineering → MLOps → Forward Deployed Engineer → GenAI System Architect
Purpose: A single sequential learning plan consolidating all eight submitted roadmaps while preserving their distinct resources, books, certifications, projects, and specialization tracks.
Starting point: QA automation/SDET experience; basic Core Java, SQL, and QA fundamentals; experience with UI/API automation and CI/CD is useful leverage.
Primary engineering stack: Java + Spring Boot + PostgreSQL.
AI/FDE stack: Python + FastAPI + Pydantic + LLM APIs + RAG/evaluation.
Full-stack stack: TypeScript + React/Next.js.
Production stack: Git, Linux, Docker, AWS, Terraform, Kubernetes when justified, CI/CD, OpenTelemetry, Prometheus/Grafana.
Study baseline: 10–12 hours/week; 12–18 months for a strong first learning cycle, with deeper expertise requiring ongoing real-world practice.
Guiding loop: Learn → Build → Test → Debug → Deploy → Monitor → Break safely → Improve → Secure → Measure → Document → Explain.
Keep every resource in this document in a backlog. Do not try to study every tool in a category at once. Learn one tool, implement a real use case, and only then compare alternatives.
PART I — HOW TO USE THE ROADMAP
Main path versus specialization tracks
Critical path
Follow the numbered phases in order. Each phase should produce a working deliverable, not just course completion.
Computer science, Git, Linux, programming foundations
Java and Python engineering fundamentals
Testing, APIs, SQL, PostgreSQL, and backend engineering
DSA, design, concurrency, and system design fundamentals
Redis, messaging, distributed systems, and microservices
Docker, CI/CD, cloud, security, observability, reliability
React/TypeScript and full-stack delivery
Math, data analysis, and classical ML
LLM engineering, RAG, evaluation, and controlled tool use
Agents/MCP, enterprise AI security and governance
MLOps, production AI, GenAI system design
FDE customer delivery and the flagship capstone
Architecture, leadership, communication, and interview readiness
Preserved specialization tracks
Study these after the relevant foundations, or when a target role calls for them:
Advanced frontend architecture and microfrontends
Enterprise data platforms, DSPM/DLP, privacy and governance
Semiconductor, C++ systems, hardware and memory validation
Advanced data engineering and lakehouse technologies
Deep cloud security, Kubernetes security and advanced SRE
Rules for choosing resources
Official documentation is the reference source; a course or book provides guided learning.
Avoid taking multiple introductory courses for the same skill. Pick one primary course, then use the other links as references.
Certifications are optional evidence of structured learning. Projects, troubleshooting, and clear explanations are stronger evidence of practical skill.
Keep a learning log: concept, implementation, failure encountered, fix, test evidence, trade-off, and next question.
Use only synthetic, public, or explicitly authorized data in portfolio projects. Never publish employer data, customer information, secrets, tokens, or confidential documents.
PHASE 0 — Computer Science, Developer Tools, and Programming Foundations
Suggested duration: Weeks 1–6, adjusted to your actual gaps.
Learn
Variables, types, expressions, conditions, loops, functions and scope
Arrays, strings, collections, files, JSON and CSV
Exceptions, debugging, logging and error handling
Compilation, runtime, stack and heap at a conceptual level
OOP, encapsulation, abstraction, composition, inheritance and polymorphism
Interfaces, immutability, cohesion, coupling and dependency injection
Git, branches, commits, merges, rebasing, pull requests and conflict resolution
Command line and Linux basics: files, permissions, processes, environment variables, pipes, signals and logs
HTTP, REST, status codes, headers, pagination, retries and timeouts
SQL joins, constraints, transactions, indexes and query plans
Big-O intuition and the difference between time and space complexity
Clean code, naming, modularity, documentation and code review
