PHASE 12 — FORWARD DEPLOYED ENGINEERING AND CUSTOMER DELIVERY
Suggested duration: Begin in parallel, then focus during the final 2–3 months.
FDE work is not just coding
A strong FDE discovers a customer problem, understands enterprise constraints, designs a solution, builds a proof of concept, deploys it, troubleshoots it, and demonstrates measurable business value.
Customer discovery and requirements
Stakeholder mapping and technical discovery workshops
Clarify business problem, users, constraints, current architecture and data flows
Separate requirements from assumptions
Decompose ambiguity into decisions, dependencies and risks
Define acceptance criteria, success metrics, POC scope and exit criteria
Turn a business problem into architecture, implementation and measurable outcome
Flow: Business Problem → Requirements → Architecture → POC → Implementation → Production → Measurement → Business Outcome
Customer deployment and integration
REST/GraphQL/webhooks/events/batch and third-party integrations
API gateway, schema evolution, retries, backoff, idempotency, pagination and rate limits
POC/prod deployment, migration, rollback, upgrades and zero-downtime strategies
Blue-green/canary, hybrid/on-premise/air-gapped environments
Proxy, private endpoint, VPN, VPC/VNet, firewall and network troubleshooting
Production handover, runbooks, monitoring, incident response and customer enablement
Executive communication and product thinking
Communicate with developers, QA, EMs, architects, CTO/CIO/CISO, PMs and business stakeholders
Architecture proposals, demos, design docs, ADRs and RFCs
Explain trade-offs, risk, cost, ROI and recommended actions
Business cases, TCO, product/customer outcomes, adoption, customer success, KPIs and OKRs
Troubleshooting: DNS/TLS/auth/API/Kafka/database/Kubernetes/CPU/memory/latency/data pipelines
Root cause analysis, 5 Whys, incident response, postmortems and corrective/preventive actions
Useful communication framework: Conclusion → Evidence → Trade-off → Recommendation → Action.
